.TH BRIDGE-RESTORE 1 "March 2026" "Bridge Restore 1.0.2-alpha" "Bridge Restore Manual"
.SH NAME
bridge-restore \- Thunderbolt Bridge connectivity manager for macOS Intel Macs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bridge-restore
[\fIOPTION\fR]
.br
.B bridge-restore
.B \-\-help
.br
.B bridge-restore
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bridge-restore
maintains a persistent Thunderbolt Bridge connection between a
.I gateway
host (typically a MacBook Pro) that shares its internet connection and one or more
.I client
hosts (such as a Mac mini Server) that receive connectivity via the bridge.
.PP
The tool implements a 7-layer OSI diagnostic engine, a 4-method Thunderbolt
physical-layer severance/re-negotiation stack, a reverse SSH tunnel manager,
an SSH ControlMaster lifecycle handler, a role-aware watchdog daemon,
a local Ollama AI fallback engine, and an optional AppleScript GUI.
.PP
Machine roles are determined by hardware UUID and stored in
.I ~/.config/bridge-restore/config.json.
All commands respect the detected role; commands that apply only to the
gateway refuse to run on a client and vice versa.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B (no arguments)
Auto-detect role and run a full restore sequence: fix bridge0 IP,
verify ARP, restore NAT rules, re-open reverse SSH tunnel,
rebuild ControlMaster.
.TP
.B \-\-status
Print current connection state across all OSI layers without taking
any remediation action.
.TP
.B \-\-diag
Run the full OSI L1–L7 diagnostic engine and print results.
Individual layers can be toggled in
.I ~/.config/bridge-restore/config.json
under the
.I diagnostics
key.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Like the default restore, but with
.B set \-x
tracing enabled for full execution visibility.
.TP
.B \-\-watchdog
Start the embedded watchdog daemon in the foreground.
The daemon polls peer reachability every 30 seconds (configurable)
and invokes restore automatically on failure.
After 10 consecutive failures it presents a macOS action menu.
.TP
.B \-\-sever
.I (Gateway only.)
Force Thunderbolt physical-layer re-negotiation using one of four
escalating software methods: bridge member remove/re-add; networksetup
service toggle; ifconfig port bounce; full bridge0 teardown/rebuild.
Exits after the first method that succeeds.
.TP
.B \-\-ai
Invoke the Ollama AI fallback engine. Runs
.B \-\-diag
internally, then submits a structured prompt to the highest-priority
available local model (preference order: llama3.2:3b, qwen2.5:3b,
gemma2:2b, gpt-oss:20b). Falls back to manual recovery instructions
if no model is available.
.TP
.B \-\-bughunt
Deploy a high-frequency bridge-state monitor to the client via the
reverse SSH tunnel, collect 5 minutes of drop-correlation data,
feed the log to the local Ollama model, and write a Markdown analysis
report to
.I ~/dev/projects/apps/app_macOS-Intel_BridgeRestore/app-logs/.
.TP
.B \-\-wizard
Launch the first-run Setup Wizard (AppleScript GUI). Configures role,
host IPs, watchdog, auto-retry, and diagnostic layers. Writes
.I ~/.config/bridge-restore/config.json.
.TP
.B \-\-prefs
Open the Preferences window (AppleScript GUI) with three tabs:
Hosts & Roles, Diagnostic Layers, Watchdog & Retry.
.TP
.B \-\-gui
Launch the full AppleScript GUI. Prompts whether to run in GUI or
CLI mode. CLI mode opens a named Terminal window; GUI mode shows the
interactive dashboard.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a command reference to stdout and exit 0.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version string and exit 0.
.SH COMPANION COMMANDS
.TP
.B tunnel-mini
.I (Gateway / MBP only.)
Full restore sequence for the gateway side: restore bridge0 to
192.168.2.1, refresh NAT rules, verify ARP for the client, bounce
the Thunderbolt port if needed, open an SSH ControlMaster to the
client via the reverse tunnel on port 2222.
.TP
.B tunnel-pro
.I (Client / Mini only.)
Full restore sequence for the client side: restore bridge0 to
192.168.2.2, bounce the Thunderbolt port if inactive, verify gateway
reachability, open the reverse SSH tunnel
.RB ( ssh\ \-R\ 2222:localhost:22\ gateway ).
.TP
.B tunnel-mini-status
Print a one-line connection summary without taking any action.
Equivalent to
.B bridge-restore \-\-status.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is stored as JSON in
.I ~/.config/bridge-restore/config.json.
.PP
Key sections:
.TP
.I my_role
\(dqgateway\(dq or \(dqclient\(dq. Auto-detected from hardware UUID if absent.
.TP
.I gateway.ip
Bridge0 IP owned by the gateway. Default: 192.168.2.1.
.TP
.I clients[0].ip
Bridge0 IP of the primary client. Default: 192.168.2.2.
.TP
.I clients[0].tunnel_port
Reverse SSH tunnel port opened on the gateway. Default: 2222.
.TP
.I diagnostics
Boolean flags for L1–L7 diagnostic layers.
.TP
.I watchdog.enabled, watchdog.interval_seconds
Watchdog on/off and check interval.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/bridge-restore/config.json
Runtime configuration.
.TP
.I ~/Library/Application\ Support/BridgeRestore/scripts/
Installed scripts (written by the pkg installer).
.TP
.I ~/Library/LaunchAgents/local.nat-persist.plist
Runs at login: restores ip_forwarding, bridge0 IP, pf NAT anchor.
.TP
.I ~/Library/LaunchAgents/local.mbp-watchdog.plist
Persistent watchdog daemon (gateway only).
.TP
.I ~/Library/LaunchAgents/local.tunnel-pro.plist
Persistent tunnel-pro restarter (client only).
.TP
.I ~/Library/LaunchAgents/local.bridge-ip.plist
Keeps bridge0 at the correct IP (client only).
.TP
.I /etc/pf.anchors/bridge-restore
Persistent NAT rules loaded by nat-persist and tunnel-mini.
.TP
.I ~/Library/Logs/BridgeRestore/
Log directory. Files: bridge-restore-app.log, mbp-watchdog.log,
tunnel-mini.log, tunnel-pro.log, nat-persist.log, bridge-ip.log.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Success.
.TP
1
Fatal error (wrong machine, unreachable gateway, tunnel failure).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Used to locate config, logs, and SSH keys.
.TP
.B PATH
Must include /usr/local/bin for companion commands.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Run a full restore (auto-detects role):
.RS
.B bridge-restore
.RE
.PP
Show current status without modifying anything:
.RS
.B bridge-restore --status
.RE
.PP
Run full OSI diagnostics:
.RS
.B bridge-restore --diag
.RE
.PP
Force Thunderbolt re-negotiation (gateway only):
.RS
.B bridge-restore --sever
.RE
.PP
Open the setup wizard on first run:
.RS
.B bridge-restore --wizard
.RE
.PP
Collect bridge-drop debugging data and analyse with local AI:
.RS
.B bridge-restore --bughunt
.RE
.SH NOTES
.PP
.B SSH tunnel architecture:
The client (mini) initiates the reverse tunnel:
.RS
.B ssh -R 2222:localhost:22 akmacks@192.168.2.1
.RE
The gateway then connects back via:
.RS
.B ssh -p 2222 akmacks@localhost
.RE
This means tunnel-pro must run on the client
.I before
tunnel-mini is run on the gateway.
.PP
.B APIPA bridge instability:
If the client's Ethernet port (BCM5722) has no driver loaded, macOS
assigns a 169.254.x.x self-assigned IP and configd enters a DHCP
re-probe loop that disturbs bridge0 every ~30 s.
Fix: sudo networksetup -setnetworkserviceenabled "Ethernet" off
.PP
.B plistlib vs PlistBuddy:
Always use Python plistlib (not PlistBuddy) to edit config.plist
files, as PlistBuddy produces corrupt ASCII-encoded plists in Tahoe.
.SH SEE ALSO
.B ssh(1), pfctl(8), networksetup(8), launchctl(1), ifconfig(8)
